<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Truc Lam Zen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trúc Lâm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Truc Lam Zen is a Vietnamese school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Zen Buddhism</a> founded at the end of the 13th century by the retired emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-nhan-tong">Tran Nhan Tong</a>, who became a monk on Mount Yen Tu. It combined Chan meditation with Confucian and native traditions and is regarded as the first school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> founded by Vietnamese rather than foreign teachers. It declined after the Tran dynasty and was revived in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/truc-lam-zen.jpg" alt="Truc Lam Zen" width="320" height="574" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vietnamese people in the Trần dynasty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%C3%A2n_dung_Ph%E1%BA%ADt_ho%C3%A0ng_Tr%E1%BA%A7n_Nh%C3%A2n_T%C3%B4ng_trong_s%C3%A1ch_Thi%E1%BB%81n_uy%E1%BB%83n_t%E1%BA%ADp_anh_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
